Definition — GoSafe stage selection (Baumann et al. 2021, Algorithm 1)
Fix a tolerance $\epsilon\gt0$, write $w=u-l$ for the confidence widths and $G$, $M$, $S$ for the current expanders, maximisers and safe set (iteration indices suppressed). At every iteration GoSafe runs

    S1 if $\max\{w(a,x_0,i):\ (a,x_0)\in G\cup M,\ i\in I\}\gt\epsilon$, where only pairs at the nominal initial state $x_0$ are considered;
    otherwise S2 if $\max\{w(a,\tilde x_0,i):\ (a,\tilde x_0)\in G,\ i\in I_g\}\gt\epsilon$;
    otherwise S3, over the pairs $(a,\tilde x_0)\notin E_f\cup S$ with $a\in A$ arbitrary and $\tilde x_0\in P_X(S):=\{\tilde x_0\in X_\mu:\ \exists a'\in A,\ (a',\tilde x_0)\in S\}$, the projection of $S$ onto the state coordinate, i.e. the grid states that already have a certified policy.

  Each stage evaluates a maximiser of its acquisition (table below); ties may be broken arbitrarily (see Primer B). After every iteration, each recorded failure state that no longer lies in the border $\partial S$ is deleted, and its pair leaves $E_f$, so it can be proposed again. There is no separate stopping test: whenever the loop is stopped, GoSafe returns $\hat a=\arg\max_{(a,x_0)\in S}l(a,x_0,0)$.

$$\begin{aligned}
    R^{\rm safe}_\epsilon(S)&=S\cup\{s\in\mathcal S\mid\exists s'\in S:\ r(s')-\epsilon-L\,d(s,s')\ge h\}, &&\text{(Lipschitz certification, eq. 1)}\\
    R^{\rm reach}(S)&=S\cup\{s\in\mathcal S\mid\exists s'\in S,\ a\in\mathcal A(s'):\ s=f(s',a)\}, &&\text{(one-step reachable, eq. 2)}\\
    R^{\rm ret}(S,\bar S)&=\bar S\cup\{s\in S\mid\exists a\in\mathcal A(s):\ f(s,a)\in\bar S\}, &&\text{(one-step returnable, eq. 3)}
    \end{aligned}$$

$$\operatorname{CVaR}_\alpha(Z)=\min_{
u\in\mathbb R}\Big\{
u+ rac{1}{1-\alpha}\,\mathbb E ig[(Z-
u)^+ ig]\Big\},$$

$$\dot h= rac{d}{dt}h(x(t))=
abla h(x)^{	op}\dot x=\underbrace{
abla h(x)^{	op}f(x)}_{L_fh(x)}+\underbrace{
abla h(x)^{	op}g(x)}_{L_gh(x)}\,u ,$$

Noise. $n_t$ is zero-mean given the history and uniformly bounded by $\sigma$ (Sui et al., via Srinivas et al.); later papers assume $\sigma$-sub-Gaussian noise, $\mathbb{E}[e^{
u n_t} \mid 	ext{history}] \le e^{
u^2\sigma^2/2}$ for all $
u \in \mathbb{R}$, which covers both noise bounded by $\sigma$ (Hoeffding's lemma) and $\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2)$ noise. The same $\sigma^2$ is used as the GP noise variance. Why: concentration of the GP mean around $f$. The history is everything known when $n_t$ is drawn: the earlier queries and observations, and the query $x_t$, which the algorithm may choose adaptively from them; "zero-mean given the history" says that none of this information predicts the next noise value on average (Primer C; sub-Gaussian variables: Primer C). Two different sigmas: the constant $\sigma$ is a known noise bound or sub-Gaussian scale (not necessarily the true standard deviation) and enters the GP as the regulariser $\lambda = \sigma^2$; the function $\sigma_t(x)$ below is the posterior standard deviation of the unknown value $f(x)$ after $t$ observations.

